# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-treatise-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 has "natural_end"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind" is false. Is S4, "So that the end and measure of this power, when in every man’s hands in the state of nature, being the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind in general, it can have no other end or measure, when in the hands of the magistrate, but to preserve the members of that society in their lives, liberties, and possessions; and so cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over their lives and fortunes, which are as much as possible to be preserved; but a power to make laws, and annex such penalties to them, as may tend to the preservation of the whole, by cutting off those parts, and those only, which are so corrupt, that they threaten the sound and healthy, without which no severity is lawful.", true then?
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 has "natural_content → natural_end" and "given_up ∧ natural_end → magistrate_end" and "magistrate_end → ¬absolute ∧ lawmaking"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "political power is the power every man has in the state of nature, given up to the society and its governors in trust that it be employed for their good and the preservation of their property", "the power every man has in the state of nature is to use such means as he thinks good and nature allows to preserve his property, and to punish breaches of the law of nature so as may most conduce to the preservation of himself and the rest of mankind", "the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind", "in the hands of the magistrate this power can have no other end or measure than to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions" and "political power is an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members of the society" are true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if the power every man has in the state of nature is to use such means as he thinks good and nature allows to preserve his property, and to punish breaches of the law of nature so as may most conduce to the preservation of himself and the rest of mankind, then the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.2 does not
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the power every man has in the state of nature is to use such means as he thinks good and nature allows to preserve his property, and to punish breaches of the law of nature so as may most conduce to the preservation of himself and the rest of mankind, then the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 takes for granted "if (political power is the power every man has in the state of nature, given up to the society and its governors in trust that it be employed for their good and the preservation of their property and the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind), then in the hands of the magistrate this power can have no other end or measure than to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 do not
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-4.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (political power is the power every man has in the state of nature, given up to the society and its governors in trust that it be employed for their good and the preservation of their property and the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind), then in the hands of the magistrate this power can have no other end or measure than to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U3: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2 takes for granted "if in the hands of the magistrate this power can have no other end or measure than to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions, then (it is not so that political power is an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members of the society and political power is a power to make laws and annex penalties tending to the preservation of the whole, cutting off only those parts so corrupt that they threaten the sound)"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 do not
locke-treatise-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-4.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if in the hands of the magistrate this power can have no other end or measure than to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions, then (it is not so that political power is an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members of the society and political power is a power to make laws and annex penalties tending to the preservation of the whole, cutting off only those parts so corrupt that they threaten the sound)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-treatise-4.md:1: argue/negation: locke-treatise-4.logic.r2.3 P3 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the end and measure of this power, in every man's hands in the state of nature, is the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind"
